By Anna Francese Gass
The New York Times. 

This simple pasta, named Pasta alla Paolina con cavolfiore, from Palermo, Italy, layers flavor upon flavor: It begins with oil-packed anchovies melted in a hot pan, then combines them with tomato sauce and a blend of cinnamon and cloves, pantry staples in Sicilian cuisine. It’s finished with a crunchy, almond-studded bread crumb mixture that comes together while the p…";}s:8:"relation";a:1:{s:10:"firstimage";s:0:"";}}s:10:"persistent";a:4:{s:4:"date";a:1:{s:7:"created";i:1685901202;}s:7:"creator";s:21:"Melissa Clepper-Faith";s:4:"user";s:3:"mcf";s:11:"last_change";a:8:{s:4:"date";i:1685901202;s:2:"ip";s:13:"71.210.56.147";s:4:"type";s:1:"C";s:2:"id";s:25:"recipes:cauliflower_pasta";s:4:"user";s:3:"mcf";s:3:"sum";s:7:"created";s:5:"extra";s:0:"";s:10:"sizechange";i:3322;}}}